      "candidates": [
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b26b7376df8f4a780706715ae0",
          "score": 0.687058687210083,
          "text": "Francis I Rákóczi\nFrancis I Rákóczi (February 24, 1645, Gyulafehérvár, Transylvania – July 8, 1676, Zboró, Royal Hungary) was a Hungarian aristocrat, elected prince of Transylvania and father of Hungarian national hero Francis Rákóczi II.Francis Rákóczi was the son of George Rákóczi II, prince of Transylvania, and Sophia Báthory. He was elected prince by the Transylvanian Diet in 1652, during his father's life. However, because of the disastrous Polish campaign of 1657 and its consequences, the Ottoman Empire removed his father from the throne in 1660, and prohibited any Rákóczi to ascend the Transylvanian throne. This left Francis unable to come by his father's legacy; he therefore withdrew to his estates in Royal Hungary.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b28bd0737189ebcaf2d104b305",
          "score": 0.6409717798233032,
          "text": "Ilona Zrínyi\nOn 1 March 1666, she married Francis Rákóczi, with whom she had three children: György, born in 1667, who died in infancy; Julianna, born in 1672; and Ferenc (commonly known as Francis Rákóczi II), born in 1676. On June 8, 1676, not long after Francis II's birth, the elder Francis died. The widowed Ilona requested guardianship of her children and was granted it, against the advice of Emperor Leopold I's advisers and against Francis I's will. In this way she also retained control over the vast Rákóczi estates, which included among them the castles of Regéc, Sárospatak, Makovica, and Munkács. In 1682 she married Imre Thököly and became an active partner in her second husband's Kuruc uprising against the Habsburgs.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b26c2370bbb438f17530520432",
          "score": 0.6272189617156982,
          "text": "Francis I Rákóczi\nGyörgy (1667)",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b26c697b3f9abe8dcf811211aa",
          "score": 0.6199358105659485,
          "text": "Francis I Rákóczi\nFrancis Rákóczi II (1676–1735)Francis II was born only three months before his father's death. He led a rebellion against Austrian rule (Rákóczi's War of Independence) and died in exile.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b26bb27d12b6e833e1959c58b0",
          "score": 0.6133710741996765,
          "text": "Francis I Rákóczi\nIn 1666 Francis married Jelena Zrinska (Hungarian: Zrínyi Ilona), a Croatian countess, and joined the Wesselényi conspiracy (Zrinski-Frankopan conspiracy in Croatia), one leader of which was Jelena's father, Petar Zrinski (Hungarian: Zrínyi Péter). Francis soon became the leader of the conspiracy, and, as a culmination of their anti-Habsburg stratagems, started an armed uprising of nobles in Upper Hungary, while the other conspirators were supposed to start the fight in Croatia. Due to poor organization and discord between the conspirators, however, the Austrian authorities were well informed; they quickly suppressed the Croatian branch of the revolt.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b26b927f37b873a54f5281cbf9",
          "score": 0.6110292673110962,
          "text": "Francis I Rákóczi\nNotably, the Rákóczi family was Calvinist, and they were staunch supporters of the Reformed Church in Hungary. However, Francis' mother, Sophia Báthory, had converted to Calvinism merely for the sake of her marriage. After her husband's death, she returned to Catholicism and supported the Counter Reformation. Francis Rákóczi also became a Catholic, thus acquiring favour with the Catholic Habsburg Court. His mother converted him to Catholicism. He was made a count in 1664.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b26bf07a368ef6fdfc42bf7f7f",
          "score": 0.5988072156906128,
          "text": "Francis I Rákóczi\nIssue",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b2754d76e3b3fa5f8b2b6dfd5e",
          "score": 0.5681025981903076,
          "text": "Ilona Zrínyi\nCountess Ilona Zrínyi (Croatian: Jelena Zrinska, Hungarian: Zrínyi Ilona) (1643, Ozalj – 18 February 1703, Izmit) was a noblewoman and heroine. She was one of the last surviving members of the Croatian-Hungarian Zrinski/Zrínyi noble family. She was the daughter of Petar Zrinski, Ban (viceroy) of Croatia, the niece of both Miklós Zrínyi and Fran Krsto Frankopan and the wife of Francis Rákóczi I and Imre Thököly, as well as the mother of Francis Rákóczi II. She is remembered in history for her Defense of Palanok Castle against the Imperial army in 1685-1688, an act for which she was regarded a heroine in Hungary.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b26c0a7398be417d37f8e55c09",
          "score": 0.5664017796516418,
          "text": "Francis I Rákóczi\nFrancis I had three  children:",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a013b26c4c772f8d31381fd12d2cfe",
          "score": 0.5554423928260803,
          "text": "Francis I Rákóczi\nJulianna Borbála (1672–1717), married Count Ferdinand Gobert von Aspremont-Lynden (1643-1708)",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        }
