        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0aa7674c08a965ba81a25082c",
          "score": 0.7146138548851013,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nHer family went on to become leaders of the Hebron Jewish community, helping unite the Sephardic and Ashkenazic Jewish communities and reaching out to the Arabic community. Her son was Rabbi Yehuda Leib Slonim, who was the father of Rebbetzin Moshke Devora Epstein, who inherited Menucha Rochel's home when she got married. Other children included Rabbi Mordechai Dov Slonim.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0a9637fbf86e079d22d9a35b3",
          "score": 0.7131743431091309,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nRebbetzin Menucha Rochel Slonim (1798–1888) was a daughter of Rabbi Dovber Schneuri, the second Rebbe of the Chabad Hasidic dynasty. She is regarded a matriarch to the Chabad dynasty as well as Hebron's Jewish population in general.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0a9ab71b38948f90682f4db48",
          "score": 0.6658892035484314,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nRebbetzin Slonim was born on the 19 Kislev, 5559 AM (27 November 1798), the same day her grandfather, Rabbi Shneur Zalman of Liadi was released from imprisonment in S. Petersburg. Her father chose the name Menucha because in Hebrew the word \"menucha\" means \"peace and quiet\". He said, \"Henceforth we shall have a little Menucha.\" She was named Rochel after an aunt that died in her youth.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0a9f072238e92c6dbe543d6fb",
          "score": 0.6554090976715088,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nEmigration to Hebron",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0a9d4769d8c62f4d21638d260",
          "score": 0.6539846658706665,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nHer husband's last name was originally Griver, a descendant of Rabbi Moses Isserles, the Rema; they chose to change it to Slonim (this was when it was still easy to change names). They then moved to Hebron.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0aa0e71098e47690b1186011f",
          "score": 0.6419776678085327,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nAfter she fell dangerously ill, her father promised that she would live to emigrate to the Land of Israel. In 1845, with the blessing of her brother-in-law, Rabbi Menachem Mendel Schneersohn, she and her family emigrated to Hebron. To allay her fears of rainstorms on the journey to Hebron, Rabbi Schneersohn blessed her to \"walk between the raindrops.\"",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0aa327f5cb00e1a88e72d705d",
          "score": 0.6323884725570679,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nLife in Hebron",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0aabd737c82fbe5dce574fd41",
          "score": 0.6283133029937744,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nAnnual visits to grave in Hebron",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0aa537a78b3a14abfeac24089",
          "score": 0.6239065527915955,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nFor forty-three years she served as the matriarch of the Hebron community. New brides and barren women would request blessings from her. Before she died on the 24th of Shevat, 5648 AM (6 February 1888), she sent a letter to the then Rebbe, Rabbi Sholom Dovber Schneersohn, informing him of her imminent passing. She thus lived during the leadership of all of the first five Lubavitcher Rebbes.She became renowned among both townspeople and beyond, Jews and non-Jews alike for her curative and salvation abilities, and was nicknamed \"the grandmother Menucha Rachel\". Every bride who visited the Tomb of Machpela on the day of her wedding came to get her blessing and the Admor Rebbe Elazar Mendel of Lelov used to visit her home three times a week when staying in Hebron. He was strict about standing when she entered or left the room and always asked her to bless him.",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        },
        {
          "document_id": "01a017b0a9837c62bac3a3d8b6b34f39",
          "score": 0.6226083636283875,
          "text": "Menucha Rochel Slonim\nOrigin of name",
          "source_hops": [
            0
          ]
        }
